            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

There is some excessive air leakage in your home that appears to be associated with a few key areas: the chimney for the current furnace, around some of the older wood windows &amp; up through the floor where there is a hole cut in the crawlspace ceiling (right below the toilet)



Upgrading the furnace will eliminate the current chimney &amp; fixing the hole below the toilet should be easy to do. Updating the older windows (the living room one in the front &amp; the 2 older wood ones in the back) may become a longer term renovation project. Overall, it would be ideal to reduce the current air leakage by about 25%. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 25% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 210&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 600 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <windows>

I have modeled an upgrade to the 3 older wood windows in your home (the living room one in the front &amp; the 2 older wood ones in the back) to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing these 3 older windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 217&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <doors>

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the front wood door to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified polyurethane insulation filled door rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend the window insert in the door be ENERGY STARÂ® qualified as well.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the front door with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified unit rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 57&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</doors>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house has a 40 USG John Wood electric hot water tank, that has an estimated energy factor of 0.83, and the cost of electricity is very high in Fort Simpson. If you switch your furnace to propane, I would recommend you consider replacing the electric hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,475&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil furnace is quite old and not very efficient, estimated to be no more than 83% Steady State Efficiency (peak performance), and it vents up a 5" chimney, I did not see a combustion air supply pipe in the furnace room. The concerning thing it the amount of soot that is coming back into the furnace room through the chimney. I suspect (as the furnace &amp; dryer are in the same closed room) the dryer is drawing combustion air (and soot) back down the chimney while the furnace is trying to operate.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE - the average efficiency over the heating season), including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This furnace will draw its' combustion air directly from outside, and vent combustion out through a sidewall pipe. This will eliminate the current chimney &amp; it will eliminate the issues the dryer is causing.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 894&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;20.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------









</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has the following ventilation:



1. The kitchen range hood vents to the outside.

2. A bathroom fan that vents to the outside.

3. The clothes dryer vents to the outside.



The kitchen range hood and dryer both seem to be operating effectively, however the bathroom fan appeared to not have good flow. You should consider replacing the bath fan with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified unit, perhaps one with an integrated humidity sensing "instant on" feature ....... or have it installed with a timer switch (5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so the fan can ensure all the excess humidity from showers is expelled &amp; then automatically turn off so heat in the house is not wasted. 



As discussed in the furnace upgrade section, it appears the air requirement for the dryer is competing with your furnace &amp; drawing combustion products &amp; soot back down the furnace chimney. This issue will be resolved if you upgrade your furnace to the one suggested.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood, the bathroom fan, and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.3 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace does vent up a chimney, however that would be eliminated if you complete the recommended furnace upgrade.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in the house. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.70 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.07 per litre 

Propane - $0.60 per litre

Wood - $367.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.35 per bag, $393.75 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Ken Baigent

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



